          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "If you develop very high thirst, frequent urination, vomiting, confusion or unusual drowsiness, seek urgent care.\n\n**What the diagnosis means:** Type 2 diabetes means your body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, the hormone that moves sugar from your blood into your cells. Your pancreas tries to make more insulin to compensate, but over time it may not keep up, so your blood sugar stays above the normal range. It usually develops gradually and often causes no symptoms at first (General clinical reference, *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*). A separate search for a plain explanation did not succeed, so this description is not sourced from that search.\n\n**First-line treatment:** Current guidance (National diabetes association standards of care) does not use one fixed sequence for everyone. The first choice depends on your other health conditions. Where no other condition changes the choice, the guidance describes metformin, a commonly used first medicine, alongside structured lifestyle changes. Kidney function is checked before and during treatment. The guidance also notes that losing about 5% of body weight and keeping it off already gives measurable benefit for blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol. I could not retrieve a diabetes-specific lifestyle passage to confirm further details. Whether a medicine suits you is a decision for you and your clinician. The diabetes association describes an HbA1c below 7% as a reasonable goal for many adults, with a stricter goal below 6.5% possibly suiting some people. Your target should be individualized, and one source notes a disagreement on this point that I could not read in full.\n\n**The code:** The coding reference lists type 2 diabetes as **E11** in ICD-10-CM, in Chapter 4 (endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases) (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*). If a record says only \"diabetes mellitus\" with no type, it defaults to type 2. Taking insulin does not change the code; insulin use is recorded separately. The code lookup tool was unavailable, and a separate search could not confirm the code from another source. Check it against your diagnosis paperwork.\n\n**Questions for your clinician:** Ask for your HbA1c and kidney results, your personal blood sugar target, the diagnosis code, and which medicines and lifestyle steps they recommend for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
